course: chinese-street
lesson: 21
title: Today, tomorrow, now, later
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Time words are built like Lego from "tiān", day: jīntiān (now-day) is today, míngtiān (bright-day) is tomorrow, zuótiān is yesterday, and hòutiān (after-day) is the day after tomorrow — a word English doesn't even have.
explain: The best one is "mǎshàng" (MAH-SHAHNG), right away, literally "on horseback": from Lín at his computer "mǎshàng!" can mean ten minutes, but from his mum with her shoes on it means NOW.
explain: Some time words are secret no's: "xiànzài bù xíng" (not now) and "yǐhòu zài shuō" (let's talk about it later) from a parent often mean "no", said softly.
explain: The day splits into zǎoshang (early morning), xiàwǔ (afternoon, literally after-noon) and wǎnshang (evening); wǎn also means "late", so "tài wǎn le" is "it's too late".
explain: Listen for tones: zuó rising is in zuótiān, yesterday, but zuò falling is "sit" or "do".
fact: "jīntiān" means today; "jīntiān wánr shénme?" is "what are we playing today?".
fact: "míngtiān" means tomorrow, as in "míngtiān jiàn" (see you tomorrow); it is not "morning", which is zǎoshang.
fact: "zuótiān" means yesterday; zuó rises, while zuò (falling) means to sit or to do.
fact: "hòutiān" means the day after tomorrow, and "qiántiān" means the day before yesterday; English has no single word for either.
fact: "xiànzài" means now; "xiànzài kěyǐ ma?" is "can we now?", and a parent's "xiànzài bù xíng" (not now) is a polite no.
fact: "mǎshàng" means right away, literally "on horseback"; "mǎshàng lái!" is "coming right now!".
fact: "yíhuìr" means in a little while; "yíhuìr jiàn" is "see you in a bit", and "děng yíhuìr" is a longer wait than "děng yíxià".
fact: "gāngcái" means just now, a moment ago, and points back; mǎshàng points forward.
fact: "yǐhòu" means later or in the future; "yǐhòu zài shuō" (let's talk later) from a parent is often a soft no.
fact: "zǎoshang" is the early morning, as in "zǎoshang qī diǎn", seven in the morning; shàngwǔ is late morning.
fact: "xiàwǔ" means afternoon, literally "after noon"; shàngwǔ, "before noon", is the late morning.
fact: "wǎnshang" means evening or night; wǎn also means late, so "tài wǎn le" is "it's too late".
exercise: Lín asks "jīntiān wánr shénme?". When does he want to play? | Today | Tomorrow | Yesterday | Next week
exercise: At the school gate Lín says "míngtiān jiàn". When will you see him? | Tomorrow | This morning | Tonight | In a minute
exercise: Lín says "zuótiān wǒ qù le gōngyuán". When did he go to the park? | Yesterday | Today | Tomorrow | The day after tomorrow
exercise: The party is "hòutiān". It's Monday now. When is the party? | Wednesday | Tuesday | Sunday | Monday
exercise: What is the day before yesterday? | qiántiān | hòutiān | zuótiān | míngtiān
exercise: Lín begs "xiànzài kěyǐ ma?". What is he asking? | Can we now? | Can we tomorrow? | Can we later? | Can we yesterday?
exercise: Lín's mum says "xiànzài bù xíng". What does she mean? | Not now — a polite no | Yes, go ahead now | Do it right away | We'll do it yesterday
exercise: What is mǎshàng word for word? | On horseback | On the bus | On the clock | On your feet
exercise: Lín's mum, shoes on, keys in hand: "mǎshàng!". What does it mean? | Right now, this second | In about an hour or so | Tomorrow morning, first thing | Whenever you like
exercise: Lín waves: "yíhuìr jiàn". When will you meet? | In a little while | Next week | Tomorrow | Never again
exercise: Which is a longer wait? | děng yíhuìr | děng yíxià | mǎshàng | xiànzài
exercise: Lín's sister asks "gāngcái nǐ qù nǎr le?". When did you go somewhere? | Just now | Tomorrow | In a minute | Next year
exercise: You ask for a puppy and Lín's dad says "yǐhòu zài shuō". What does it usually mean? | A soft no: we'll talk later | Yes, we'll get one tomorrow | Yes, right now, let's go | Ask your granny instead
exercise: School starts at "zǎoshang bā diǎn". When is that? | Eight in the morning | Eight in the evening | Eight in the afternoon | Eighteen o'clock
exercise: After lunch, after school — what part of the day is that? | xiàwǔ | zǎoshang | wǎnshang | shàngwǔ
exercise: Lín's mum asks "wǎnshang chī shénme?". When is she asking about? | This evening, dinner | This morning, breakfast | Tomorrow lunch | Yesterday's tea
exercise: Lín wants to play at 10 pm and his dad says "tài wǎn le". What does he mean? | It's too late | It's too early | It's too cold | It's too far
test: my mate thinks míngtiān means "morning" cos "míng" sounds like morning. right? | No, míngtiān is tomorrow; morning is zǎoshang | Yes, míngtiān is morning, he's spot on | Yes, it means "this morning" | No, míngtiān means yesterday, the day before
test: Lín says he's coming "mǎshàng" from his game. Twenty minutes later he's still playing. What's the joke? | From a kid at a screen, mǎshàng stretches | mǎshàng really means twenty minutes exactly | mǎshàng means "never" | He meant "yesterday", by mistake
test: Which word points back in time, and which forward? | gāngcái back, mǎshàng forward | mǎshàng back, gāngcái forward | Both point back | Both point forward
test: Lín says "wǒ zuò tiān" with a falling zuò. What sounds wrong? | zuò falling is "sit, do", not yesterday | Nothing, both tones mean yesterday | zuò means tomorrow | zuò means today
test: It's Friday. Lín says the sleepover is hòutiān. Which day is it? | Sunday | Saturday | Monday | Thursday
test: why does English need four words "the day after tomorrow" when Chinese has one? | English just has no single word for it | Chinese is a shorter language overall | English is older, so its words are long | Chinese has no word either
test: In a cartoon a boy asks "can I have sweets?", and his mum says "yǐhòu zài shuō". He cheers. Was he right to cheer? | Probably not, it's usually a soft no | Yes, it means "yes, have them now" | Yes, it means "take two" | No, it means "never ever, not once"
test: Lín's granny says "shàngwǔ" about the dentist. When is it? | Late morning, before noon | Late afternoon | Evening | Early dawn
test: What would happen if you told Lín "zǎoshang jiàn" at home time on Monday? | He'd think you'll meet in the morning | He'd think you'll meet tonight | He'd think you never want to meet | He'd think it's Sunday
test: Which time word hides "after noon" inside it? | xiàwǔ | wǎnshang | zǎoshang | jīntiān
test: Lín's dad says "jīntiān tài wǎn le, míngtiān ba". What's the plan? | Too late today; tomorrow then | Right now, today | Yesterday was too late | Next week, maybe
exam: Identify the Mandarin term denoting the day subsequent to the morrow. | hòutiān | qiántiān | zuótiān | míngtiān
exam: It is given that "mǎshàng" is employed by a parent who stands ready to depart. Select the interpretation consistent with the established usage. | Immediately, without delay | At an indeterminate future date | On the following day | Not at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the parental utterance "yǐhòu zài shuō"? | It is frequently a courteous deferral amounting to refusal | It is an unconditional grant of permission for the request | It is an enquiry as to the hour | It is a greeting upon arrival
exam: Ascertain the literal composition of the term "xiàwǔ", signifying afternoon. | After noon | Before noon | Late night | Early morning
